      "data": {
        "text": "**Defining API Key Source**\n\nOkay, I'm zeroing in on the architecture of `fetch_api_key.py`. I've decided to pull the API key from a managed secrets store, and I'm leaning toward using AWS as the provider, considering its widespread use. Now, I need to figure out how to best integrate with the AWS secrets manager.\n\n\n**Implementing Key Retrieval**\n\nI've got the `fetch_api_key.py` file sketched out. It leverages `boto3` to interact with AWS Secrets Manager. For demonstration, I'm fetching a secret named `my_api_key_secret` from the `us-east-1` region. Of course, these parameters will need to be configured for any real-world deployment. I'm focusing on the core logic now.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789820762438,
          "start": 1789820759370
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0b9a16d4a001PFYXk7dmOXG7Lj",
      "message_id": "msg_0b9a167da001EL9svOEg8E11yu",
      "session_id": "ses_f465e98a6ffeJmZolfYtrsMDKn",
      "time_created": 1789820759370,
      "time_updated": 1789820762438
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "callID": "QVyWMYdfFjvnTYr2",
        "metadata": {
          "google": {
            "thoughtSignature": ""
          }
        },
        "state": {
          "input": {
            "content": "\nimport boto3\nimport os\n\ndef fetch_api_key(secret_name, region_name=\"us-east-1\"):\n    \"\"\"\n    Fetches an API key from AWS Secrets Manager.\n\n    Args:\n        secret_name (str): The name of the secret to retrieve.\n        region_name (str): The AWS region where the secret is stored.\n\n    Returns:\n        str: The retrieved API key.\n        None: If the secret cannot be retrieved.\n    \"\"\"\n    try:\n        # Create a Secrets Manager client\n        client = boto3.client(\"secretsmanager\", region_name=region_name)\n\n        # Retrieve the secret value\n        get_secret_value_response = client.get_secret_value(SecretId=secret_name)\n\n        # Decrypts secret using the associated KMS key.\n        # Depending on whether the secret is a string or binary, one of these fields will be populated.\n        if \"SecretString\" in get_secret_value_response:\n            return get_secret_value_response[\"SecretString\"]\n        else:\n            # Handle binary secrets if needed, but for API keys, SecretString is common\n            return None\n    except Exception as e:\n        print(f\"Error fetching secret '{secret_name}': {e}\")\n        return None\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    # Example usage:\n    # Set your secret name and region\n    api_secret_name = os.environ.get(\"API_SECRET_NAME\", \"my_api_key_secret\")\n    aws_region = os.environ.get(\"AWS_REGION\", \"us-east-1\")\n\n    api_key = fetch_api_key(api_secret_name, aws_region)\n\n    if api_key:\n        print(\"API Key fetched successfully (first 20 characters):\", api_key[:20] + \"...\")\n    else:\n        print(\"Failed to fetch API Key.\")\n",
            "filePath": "fetch_api_key.py"
          },
